Q: Jenkins + Docker: How to control docker user when using Image.inside command A: <p>I found you can actually change user by adding <code>args</code> like following. Although -u 1000:1000 will still be there in the <code>docker run</code>, you will add an additional -u [your user] after 1000:1000. Docker will use the latest -u parameter</p>
<pre><code>agent {
  docker {
    image 'your image'
    args '-u root --privileged'
  }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Remove selected value shadcn A: <ul>
<li><p><strong>Intercept <code>onPointerDown</code>:</strong> Call <code>e.stopPropagation()</code> on <code>onPointerDown</code> so the event does not reach the Radix trigger and open the component.<br />
<a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Element/pointerdown_event" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Element/pointerdown_event</a></p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Keep accessibility intact:</strong> Use an accessible with <code>role=&quot;button&quot;</code> and <code>tabIndex={0}</code> instead of a nested, and handle both click and keyboard (<code>Enter</code> / <code>Space</code>) events.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Restore the placeholder:</strong> Reset the controlled value to <code>&quot;&quot;</code> so Radix treats the field as empty and displays the placeholder again.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;span
  role=&quot;button&quot;
  tabIndex={0}
  onPointerDown={(e) =&gt; { e.stopPropagation(); }}
  onClick={(e) =&gt; { e.stopPropagation(); onClear?.(); }}
  onKeyDown={(e) =&gt; { if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') { e.stopPropagation(); e.preventDefault(); onClear?.(); } }}
&gt;
  &lt;X className=&quot;h-4 w-4&quot; /&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;
</code></pre>
</li>
</ul>

Q: Calculating transform CSS params depending on the child&#39;s index A: <p>I would prefer using a CSS variable:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>/* somewhere in base.css to number of maximum children you want to handle, could be generated with SASS for example */

:nth-child(1){
  --child-index: 1;
}

:nth-child(2){
  --child-index: 2;
}

/* your current CSS */
li{
  transform: translateX(calc(var(--child-index)*10%))
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;ul&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;I'm the first child&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;I'm the second child&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p><strong>2026 UPDATE</strong></p>
<p><a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Values/sibling-index" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/Reference/Values/sibling-index</a></p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>li{
  transform: translateX(calc(sibling-index() * 10%))
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;ul&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;I'm the first child&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;li&gt;I'm the second child&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: Android approach to launch an emergency Activity from background without user interaction A: <p>You are allowed to launch activities from the background upon receiving a message (or any data layer event I believe) from the <a href="https://developer.android.com/training/wearables/data/data-items#ListenEvents" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Data Layer API</a>. In my case, I use a foreground service as a proxy to launch the activity:</p>
<pre><code>class WearableSyncService : WearableListenerService() {
    override fun onMessageReceived(message: MessageEvent) {
        // AlarmService is a foreground `Service`
        val serviceIntent = Intent(this, AlarmService::class.java)
        ContextCompat.startForegroundService(this, serviceIntent)
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>I'm not sure if a foreground service proxy is required to launch the activity, you may want to experiment with that.</p>

Q: How can I block specific keyboard keys using python&#39;s keyboard module? A: <pre><code>import threading , keyboard
# te clado
def teclas():
    teclas_bloqueadas = []

    # bloquea el teclado
    for y in range(1,999):
        try:
            keyboard.block_key(y)
            teclas_bloqueadas.append(y)
        except:
            pass

    # Bucle de monitoreo (sí detecta el archivo)
    while True:
        if os.path.exists(&quot;D:/llave.txt&quot;):
            # Desbloquear el teclado
            for y in teclas_bloqueadas:
                try:
                    keyboard.unblock_key(y)
                except:
                    pass
        time.sleep(1)


# daemon=True sierra  el teclado
teclass = threading.Thread(target=teclas , daemon=True) 
teclass.start()
</code></pre>
